$DIR/main.p:18:2: ticks++ uses the per-CPU variable ticks outside a region with interrupts disabled, which it needs because the interrupt handler timer also uses it
    ticks has one copy for each processor. This is a compound assignment or
    increment, which reads and writes it, so the task could move to another
    processor in the middle of it and read one processor's copy and write
    another's. The interrupt handler timer also uses ticks, so an interrupt on
    this processor could run in the middle of it as well: it must be inside a
    region with maskable interrupts disabled (x86.Cli to x86.Sti,
    x86.SaveFlagsCli to x86.RestoreFlags, or an rt.Lock held), not just with
    preemption disabled. A lone load or store of a whole variable of at most a
    machine word is allowed anywhere.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
    state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor
    and need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most
    common use (a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.
    Put the access inside a region with interrupts disabled:
        f := x86.SaveFlagsCli()
        ticks++
        x86.RestoreFlags(f)
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2022" for a longer explanation)
